/*
  Arabic purpose strings for the packaged Info.plist.

  Arabic is a first-class product language, not a translation afterthought: the
  Flutter catalogue ships lib/l10n/arb/app_ar.arb and the application name in
  Arabic is كرار. A system prompt that stays English while the rest of the
  application is Arabic is the point at which a user stops reading it, and a
  purpose string nobody reads is a privacy disclosure that has not been made.

  The spelling is كرار, not قرار. Both are pronounceable readings of the Latin
  name, but قرار is the Arabic word "decision" and is the legacy system's name
  — documentation-style-guide.md reserves "Qarar" for that system and this
  platform is "Karar". The two were mixed here: the launcher said قرار while
  twelve in-app messages said كرار, so a user met one name on the home screen
  and another in a security notice. `brand_name_test.dart` now holds them
  together.

  This is written as Arabic, not as a word-for-word rendering of the English.
  It carries the same four facts: the unlock is local to this device, the
  device performs the comparison, Karar neither receives nor stores the face
  data, and unlocking does not stand in for signing in.
*/
"NSFaceIDUsageDescription" = "يفتح Face ID تطبيق كرار على هذا الجهاز. تتم المطابقة داخل جهازك، ولا يتلقى كرار بيانات وجهك ولا يخزّنها. وفتح التطبيق لا يغني عن تسجيل الدخول.";
